window.VOLUME_DOCS={"v01": {"title": "Thiel's Law", "lede": "Peter Thiel on getting the foundation right — Campus Connect Academy", "toc": ["About this volume", "Concept 1: Zero to one, not one to n", "Concept 2: Question the lessons everyone learned", "Concept 3: Competition is for losers", "Concept 4: Last mover advantage", "Concept 5: Definite optimism and the power law", "Concept 6: Great companies are built on secrets", "Concept 7: Thiel's Law: get the foundation right", "Concept 8: Distribution matters, and seven questions every business must answer", "Put it to work"], "html": "<section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s1\" data-label=\"About this volume\"><h2>About this volume</h2><p>The core lesson of Zero to One is that great companies create something new and own it, and that the choices made at the very start decide whether they can. This volume walks through the book&#x27;s eight biggest ideas and what each one means for a student founder.</p><p>About the series. The Founder&#x27;s Shelf is a Campus Connect Academy series. Each volume takes one essential business book and turns it into practical lessons for student founders. Read them in order: they follow a founder from idea, to customer, to sale, to funding, to team, to habits.</p><p>About the author. Peter Thiel co-founded PayPal and Palantir, founded the venture firm Founders Fund, and was Facebook&#x27;s first outside investor. Zero to One (Crown Business, 2014), written with Blake Masters, grew out of a startup course Thiel taught at Stanford in 2012. Masters was a student in the class, and his detailed notes became the basis of the book.</p><p>How to read this doc. Each section covers one concept from the book, with the chapter it comes from so you can go to the source. The explanations are our summaries in our own words. Direct quotes from the book are kept short and marked with quotation marks and a chapter reference. This doc is a study guide, not a substitute: the book itself is short (about 200 pages) and worth reading in full.</p><p>Each concept ends with For student founders, a short note on how to apply it right now, while you&#x27;re still in school.</p></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s2\" data-label=\"Concept 1: Zero to one, not one to n\"><h2>Concept 1: Zero to one, not one to n</h2><p>Chapter 1, &quot;The Challenge of the Future&quot;</p><p>Thiel&#x27;s central claim is that there are two kinds of progress, and startups should aim for the harder one.</p><ul><li>Horizontal progress (1 to n) copies things that already work and spreads them. Taking one typewriter and building a hundred is horizontal. Thiel&#x27;s big-picture example is globalization: spreading existing technology around the world.</li><li>Vertical progress (0 to 1) does something new. Going from a typewriter to a word processor is vertical. Thiel calls this technology in the broadest sense: any new and better way of doing things.</li></ul><p>His argument is that copying is easier, but it only redistributes value that already exists. Creating something new is what produces outsized value, both for the world and for the company that does it. Every great company happens only once: the next Mark Zuckerberg won&#x27;t build a social network, and the next Larry Page won&#x27;t build a search engine. If you&#x27;re copying them, you&#x27;re not learning from them.</p><h3>The contrarian question</h3><p>Thiel opens the book with the question he likes to ask in job interviews:</p><p>&quot;What important truth do very few people agree with you on?&quot; (Ch. 1)</p><p>He explains that it&#x27;s hard to answer for two reasons. It&#x27;s intellectually hard, because most of what we learn in school is knowledge everyone already accepts. It&#x27;s psychologically hard, because saying something unpopular out loud takes courage. A good answer has the form &quot;Most people believe X, but the truth is the opposite of X.&quot; Thiel connects this directly to startups: a good business is built on a truth about the future that others haven&#x27;t seen yet.</p><p>He also warns against a weak version of the answer. Being contrarian is not the same as disagreeing with the crowd for its own sake. The goal is to think for yourself, and sometimes that leads you somewhere others haven&#x27;t gone.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Ask yourself whether your idea is 0 to 1 or 1 to n. &quot;Uber for X&quot; or &quot;a better version of an app that exists&quot; is usually 1 to n. That isn&#x27;t automatically bad, but you should know which one you&#x27;re building.</li><li>Write down your own answer to the contrarian question. If you can&#x27;t finish the sentence &quot;Most people believe X, but actually...&quot; about your market, you may not have found your edge yet.</li><li>Use the question with co-founders and early hires. How someone answers tells you a lot about how independently they think.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s3\" data-label=\"Concept 2: Question the lessons everyone learned\"><h2>Concept 2: Question the lessons everyone learned</h2><p>Chapter 2, &quot;Party Like It&#x27;s 1999&quot;</p><p>Thiel argues that Silicon Valley learned the wrong lessons from the dot-com crash of 2000, and that the opposite lessons are often closer to the truth.</p><p>After the bubble burst, founders and investors settled on four rules that felt safe. Thiel&#x27;s position is that these rules turned into dogma, and that following them blindly keeps founders thinking small. The table below sets the conventional lesson next to Thiel&#x27;s counter-principle.</p><table><tr><td>What the Valley took away (our summary)</td><td>Thiel&#x27;s counter-argument (our summary)</td></tr><tr><td>Take only small, safe steps</td><td>A bold attempt beats a trivial one</td></tr><tr><td>Don&#x27;t commit to a plan; stay flexible</td><td>Having a flawed plan beats drifting with none</td></tr><tr><td>Win by beating existing rivals</td><td>Crowded, competitive markets wipe out profit</td></tr><tr><td>Build a great product and sales will follow</td><td>Selling is as important as building</td></tr></table><p>He doesn&#x27;t say the old lessons are always wrong. His point is that they were a reaction to one specific disaster, and a reaction isn&#x27;t a strategy. A founder who only takes small, safe steps never attempts anything that could matter much.</p><p>The &quot;bad plan is better than no plan&quot; idea is the one students push back on most. Thiel&#x27;s reasoning is that iterating without a direction tends to produce small, random improvements. A plan, even an imperfect one, gives you something to test and revise on purpose. This sets up a bigger argument he makes in Chapter 6 about definite thinking.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Look at the advice you hear most often in startup programs, such as &quot;just ship it&quot; or &quot;pivot fast.&quot; Ask whether it fits your situation or whether you&#x27;re following it because everyone says it.</li><li>Write a one-page plan for where your company should be in three years. You&#x27;ll change it, but having one forces you to make decisions.</li><li>Don&#x27;t treat sales as an afterthought. Concept 8 covers why distribution can decide whether a good product survives.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s4\" data-label=\"Concept 3: Competition is for losers\"><h2>Concept 3: Competition is for losers</h2><p>Chapters 3–4, &quot;All Happy Companies Are Different&quot; and &quot;The Ideology of Competition&quot;</p><p>Thiel&#x27;s most famous and most provocative idea is that founders should try to escape competition, not win it. He made the same case in a 2014 Wall Street Journal essay titled &quot;Competition Is for Losers.&quot;</p><h3>Perfect competition vs. monopoly</h3><p>Economics classes teach perfect competition as the ideal: many firms selling the same thing, with prices pushed down until no one earns much profit. Thiel points out what that means for a business owner. In a perfectly competitive market, you&#x27;re fighting just to survive, so you can&#x27;t plan long-term or invest in anything ambitious.</p><p>A creative monopoly, in Thiel&#x27;s sense, is a company so much better at what it does that no one offers a close substitute. That company earns real profits, and those profits let it think long-term, treat employees well and keep innovating. Thiel&#x27;s main example is Google, which earned large profits in search while rival search engines barely mattered.</p><p>He&#x27;s careful about what he means. He is not defending companies that win through government favors or illegal tactics. He&#x27;s describing companies that become dominant by being much better.</p><h3>Everyone lies about their market</h3><p>Thiel notes that both kinds of companies distort how they describe their market, in opposite directions:</p><ul><li>Monopolies pretend to be small. They describe their market as a big union of many markets, so their share looks tiny and they avoid attention. Google, for example, might describe itself as an advertising or technology company rather than as the dominant search engine.</li><li>Competitive businesses pretend to be unique. They describe their market as a narrow intersection of categories. His example is a restaurant that calls itself &quot;the only British restaurant in Palo Alto,&quot; when the real market is all restaurants in town.</li></ul><p>Students should watch for the second lie in their own pitch. Defining your market as an intersection so narrow that you have no competitors fools only you.</p><h3>Why we compete anyway</h3><p>In Chapter 4, Thiel argues that competition is partly psychological. School trains people to compete for the same grades, the same schools and the same jobs. Rivals then focus on each other instead of on what&#x27;s valuable. His PayPal example: in 1999 and 2000, PayPal and Elon Musk&#x27;s X.com were locked in a costly fight. Merging the two let them survive the dot-com crash.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>If your pitch includes a slide with ten competitors, ask what you&#x27;d do so much better that customers wouldn&#x27;t consider them.</li><li>Check your market definition. Is it a real market people pay for, or a narrow combination of labels?</li><li>Notice when you&#x27;re choosing a path (a major, an internship, a startup idea) mainly because others are competing for it.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s5\" data-label=\"Concept 4: Last mover advantage\"><h2>Concept 4: Last mover advantage</h2><p>Chapter 5, &quot;Last Mover Advantage&quot;</p><p>Being first to market matters less than being the company that still dominates in ten or twenty years. Thiel calls this being the last mover: the one who makes the final big improvement in a market and then holds it.</p><p>His reasoning comes from finance. A company is worth the cash it will make in the future, so durability matters more than early growth. Many fast-growing startups are worth little because they can&#x27;t hold their position. A slower company with a lasting edge can be worth far more.</p><h3>Four sources of a lasting edge</h3><p>Thiel names four characteristics that make a monopoly durable. Most strong companies combine several.</p><table><tr><td>Source</td><td>What it means (our summary)</td><td>Example from the book</td></tr><tr><td>Proprietary technology</td><td>Tech so much better that it&#x27;s hard to copy. Thiel&#x27;s rule of thumb is roughly 10 times better than the closest substitute on an important dimension.</td><td>Google&#x27;s search algorithm</td></tr><tr><td>Network effects</td><td>The product gets more useful as more people use it, so the early users must find it valuable even when the network is small.</td><td>Facebook&#x27;s start at Harvard</td></tr><tr><td>Economies of scale</td><td>Costs per customer fall as the business grows, which is easiest for software, where copies are almost free.</td><td>Twitter and other software businesses</td></tr><tr><td>Branding</td><td>A strong, distinct brand people trust, built on real substance rather than image alone.</td><td>Apple</td></tr></table><h3>Start small, then scale</h3><p>Thiel&#x27;s building plan has three steps:</p><ul><li>Start small and monopolize. Pick a market small enough that you can dominate it quickly. A small group of people who badly need your product beats a huge market where you&#x27;re one option among many. Amazon started with books; PayPal focused early on high-volume eBay sellers.</li><li>Scale up into nearby markets. Once you own the first niche, expand into related markets one ring at a time. Amazon moved from books to other media and then to everything.</li><li>Don&#x27;t obsess over disruption. Thiel thinks &quot;disruption&quot; makes founders define themselves against incumbents, which invites a fight. It&#x27;s usually better to create new value than to aim at destroying someone else&#x27;s.</li></ul><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Campus is a natural first niche. A product that one school badly needs can be dominated before you expand, which is how Facebook began.</li><li>Ask which of the four sources of durability your business could build over time, and which one you could start building now.</li><li>Test the 10x rule honestly. If your product is only slightly better, customers have little reason to switch.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s6\" data-label=\"Concept 5: Definite optimism and the power law\"><h2>Concept 5: Definite optimism and the power law</h2><p>Chapters 6–7, &quot;You Are Not a Lottery Ticket&quot; and &quot;Follow the Money&quot;</p><p>Thiel argues that success is less random than people think, and that results in startups are extremely uneven. Together, these two ideas mean founders should make deliberate plans and concentrate on the few things that matter most.</p><h3>Is the future something you design?</h3><p>Thiel sorts attitudes about the future on two questions. Do you expect the future to be better or worse? And do you think it can be planned (definite) or that it&#x27;s random (indefinite)? That gives four outlooks. The examples are Thiel&#x27;s broad generalizations, not precise history.</p><table><tr><td></td><td>Future can be planned (definite)</td><td>Future is random (indefinite)</td></tr><tr><td>Future will be better (optimist)</td><td>Build ambitious plans to shape it. Thiel&#x27;s example: the US in the 1950s and 1960s, with big projects like the space program.</td><td>Expect things to improve but don&#x27;t plan how. Thiel says this describes the US since the 1980s, focused on finance and keeping options open.</td></tr><tr><td>Future will be worse (pessimist)</td><td>Prepare carefully for a hard future. Thiel&#x27;s example: China, copying proven models as fast as possible.</td><td>Expect decline and do little about it. Thiel&#x27;s example: much of Europe.</td></tr></table><p>Thiel wants founders to be definite optimists. His critique of the indefinite mindset is that it produces people who collect options and credentials without committing to anything. He applies this to students too: building a resume of many unrelated achievements keeps doors open but doesn&#x27;t build anything deep.</p><p>He also pushes back on the idea that startup success is mostly luck. He doesn&#x27;t deny luck exists. His claim is that treating success as a lottery leads people to stop planning, and that companies with a clear long-term plan are undervalued in a world that treats the future as random.</p><h3>The power law</h3><p>In Chapter 7, Thiel explains that startup outcomes follow a power law, not a normal bell curve. A tiny number of companies produce most of the value, and the rest produce little or nothing.</p><p>He illustrates it with venture capital. In a successful venture fund, the single best investment often returns as much as, or more than, all the others combined. For Founders Fund, Thiel notes that the Facebook investment alone returned more than the rest of its 2005 fund put together. That&#x27;s why good investors only back companies they believe could return the entire fund.</p><p>Thiel applies the power law to individuals too. Within a business, one market, one channel or one strategy usually matters far more than the others. In a career, it&#x27;s better to commit deeply to something you&#x27;re good at that could become very valuable than to spread yourself thin.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Look at your own activities. Are you building something specific, or collecting credentials to keep options open?</li><li>When you prioritize, look for the one thing that would matter more than everything else combined: one customer segment, one feature, one channel.</li><li>If you pitch investors, understand the power law from their side. They&#x27;re asking whether you could become the outlier, not whether you&#x27;ll be a modest success.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s7\" data-label=\"Concept 6: Great companies are built on secrets\"><h2>Concept 6: Great companies are built on secrets</h2><p>Chapter 8, &quot;Secrets&quot;</p><p>Thiel believes there are still important truths no one has discovered, and that every great company is built on one. A secret, in his sense, is something true and valuable that most people don&#x27;t yet know or believe.</p><h3>Three kinds of knowledge</h3><p>Thiel sorts what we could know into three kinds:</p><ul><li>Conventions: things that are easy to learn and that everyone already knows.</li><li>Secrets: things that are hard to figure out but possible with effort.</li><li>Mysteries: things that are impossible to know.</li></ul><p>He argues that many people now act as if only conventions and mysteries exist. If everything worth knowing is either already known or unknowable, there&#x27;s no reason to search. Thiel thinks that belief is false, and that it holds back both science and business.</p><h3>Why people stop looking</h3><p>Thiel names four trends that discourage people from hunting for secrets. Each one is a short label for a longer argument in the chapter:</p><ul><li>Incrementalism: school and careers reward doing exactly what&#x27;s expected, one small step at a time.</li><li>Risk aversion: people fear being wrong in public, and a secret by definition looks wrong to most people at first.</li><li>Complacency: people at the top of institutions can succeed without new ideas, so they stop looking.</li><li>Flatness: in a connected, global world, people assume someone smarter has surely already found any good idea.</li></ul><h3>Where to look</h3><p>Thiel suggests two places: secrets about nature (science and technology) and secrets about people (what others don&#x27;t know about themselves or hide). He points to companies that built on something that seemed obvious only afterward. Airbnb saw that many people had unused space and would host strangers. Ride-sharing companies saw that people would get into strangers&#x27; cars if the system created enough trust.</p><p>He also advises founders to be careful whom they share a secret with. Tell the people you need to, such as co-founders and early team, rather than announcing it to everyone.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Look where others aren&#x27;t looking: areas no one is studying, problems everyone treats as normal, and markets people consider too small or too strange.</li><li>Your own experience can be a source. As a student, you see things about campus life, your major or your community that outsiders don&#x27;t.</li><li>Keep a running &quot;secrets list&quot;: things you&#x27;ve noticed that are true but that most people around you don&#x27;t believe or haven&#x27;t noticed.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s8\" data-label=\"Concept 7: Thiel&#x27;s Law: get the foundation right\"><h2>Concept 7: Thiel&#x27;s Law: get the foundation right</h2><p>Chapters 9–10, &quot;Foundations&quot; and &quot;The Mechanics of Mafia&quot;</p><p>The idea this volume is named for comes from Chapter 9. Thiel states it as a rule:</p><p>&quot;A startup messed up at its foundation cannot be fixed.&quot; (Ch. 9)</p><p>His point is that early decisions harden. Who your co-founders are, who owns what, who sits on the board, and how people are paid are hard to undo later. A bad product feature can be rebuilt. A broken founding team or a messy ownership structure usually can&#x27;t.</p><h3>Choosing co-founders</h3><p>Thiel compares picking a co-founder to getting married. Talent matters, but so does how well you work together and how much history you share. He notes that conflict between founders is one of the most common reasons startups fail, and that people should know each other well before starting a company together.</p><h3>Ownership, possession and control</h3><p>Thiel separates three things that founders often mix up:</p><ul><li>Ownership: who legally holds the company&#x27;s equity.</li><li>Possession: who actually runs the company day to day.</li><li>Control: who formally governs it, mainly the board of directors.</li></ul><p>Problems start when these are badly misaligned, for example when investors control a company the founders run, or when people own large stakes but aren&#x27;t working on the company.</p><p>His practical guidance, summarized:</p><ul><li>Keep the board small. Thiel recommends three directors as ideal for a startup, and no more than five until a company goes public. Small boards communicate better and can actually hold management accountable.</li><li>Go all in. Everyone involved should generally be full-time. Part-time founders, remote contractors and advisers who are only half-committed weaken alignment.</li><li>Pay in equity, not cash. Thiel argues that a startup CEO who takes a low salary stays aligned with the company&#x27;s long-term value. Equity ties everyone to the future, while cash rewards the present.</li><li>Vest over time. Standard equity vests over several years, so people earn their stake by staying and contributing.</li></ul><h3>Culture: the &quot;PayPal Mafia&quot;</h3><p>Chapter 10 is about building a team that sticks together. The early PayPal team became known as the &quot;PayPal Mafia&quot; because so many of them went on to found or fund major companies, including YouTube, LinkedIn, Yelp, Palantir and SpaceX.</p><p>Thiel&#x27;s lessons from that experience:</p><ul><li>Culture is not perks. Free food and fun offices don&#x27;t create a culture. Culture is the team itself and what it believes.</li><li>Recruit with a mission. Ask why a talented person would join your company instead of a big, well-paying one. The answer has to be about your specific mission and your specific team, not about generic benefits.</li><li>Give everyone one clear job. At PayPal, Thiel says, giving each employee one distinct responsibility cut down on internal conflict, because people weren&#x27;t fighting over the same territory.</li><li>Be closer to a cult than a consultancy. Thiel describes a range from cults, where members believe something intensely and wrongly, to consultants, who have no deep commitment to anything. He thinks the best startups sit toward the committed end: they believe something important and true that outsiders miss.</li></ul><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Before you start a company with friends, talk through the hard questions: who does what, how equity is split, what happens if someone leaves or stops contributing.</li><li>Put agreements in writing early, including vesting. It feels awkward among friends, but it&#x27;s much harder to fix later.</li><li>Be wary of adding many part-time &quot;co-founders&quot; or advisers with equity. Each one is a foundation decision.</li><li>Write down your mission in one or two sentences, and test whether it would convince a strong candidate to choose you over a big-company internship.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s9\" data-label=\"Concept 8: Distribution matters, and seven questions every business must answer\"><h2>Concept 8: Distribution matters, and seven questions every business must answer</h2><p>Chapters 11 and 13, &quot;If You Build It, Will They Come?&quot; and &quot;Seeing Green&quot;</p><p>A great product with no way to reach customers fails. Thiel argues that engineers and technical founders tend to underestimate sales, partly because good selling is meant to look effortless and so it&#x27;s easy to miss.</p><h3>The math of distribution</h3><p>Thiel frames distribution around two numbers: how much a customer is worth to you over time (customer lifetime value) and how much it costs to win that customer (customer acquisition cost). A business works only if the first is meaningfully larger than the second.</p><p>He lays out a spectrum of ways to sell, from expensive to cheap per customer:</p><table><tr><td>Method</td><td>Typical deal</td><td>How it works (our summary)</td></tr><tr><td>Complex sales</td><td>Very large contracts</td><td>A founder or senior leader personally closes a few big deals, often with businesses or governments.</td></tr><tr><td>Personal sales</td><td>Mid-sized deals</td><td>A sales team sells directly to customers, one relationship at a time.</td></tr><tr><td>Marketing and advertising</td><td>Small purchases, many customers</td><td>Ads and campaigns reach large audiences for low-priced products.</td></tr><tr><td>Viral marketing</td><td>Free or very cheap</td><td>Users bring in more users because using the product invites others. PayPal grew this way by paying sign-up bonuses.</td></tr></table><p>Thiel warns about a dead zone in the middle: products too expensive to sell with cheap ads but too cheap to justify a salesperson. Many startups get stuck there.</p><p>He applies the power law here too. Usually one distribution channel works far better than all the others, so the job is to find that one and concentrate on it.</p><p>Finally, Thiel stresses that founders are always selling, not just to customers. They sell to employees when recruiting, to investors when fundraising and to the press when building a reputation.</p><h3>The seven questions</h3><p>In Chapter 13, Thiel looks at why many clean-energy startups of the late 2000s failed. He concludes that every business should be able to answer seven questions, and that the failed companies answered few or none of them well.</p><ul><li>Engineering: Can you build a breakthrough rather than a small improvement?</li><li>Timing: Is now the right moment to start this business?</li><li>Monopoly: Are you starting with a large share of a small market?</li><li>People: Do you have the right team?</li><li>Distribution: Do you have a way not just to create a product but to deliver it?</li><li>Durability: Will your position still be defensible in 10 and 20 years?</li><li>Secret: Have you found a unique opportunity others don&#x27;t see?</li></ul><p>The questions are our phrasing of Thiel&#x27;s list, and they tie together the whole book. Engineering echoes Concept 1, monopoly echoes Concepts 3 and 4, durability echoes Concept 4, people echoes Concept 7, distribution is this concept, and secret is Concept 6.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Estimate your lifetime value and acquisition cost now, even roughly. Students often learn here that their pricing can&#x27;t support the way they planned to sell.</li><li>Pick your one most promising channel, whether that&#x27;s campus ambassadors, Instagram, partnerships with clubs or direct sales, and test it seriously before adding others.</li><li>Score your startup on the seven questions from 1 to 5. Your lowest scores show you what to work on next.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s10\" data-label=\"Put it to work\"><h2>Put it to work</h2><p>The exercises below turn this volume&#x27;s ideas into concrete next steps. Most take under an hour.</p><h3>Student founder action plan</h3><ul><li>☐ Answer the contrarian question (Concept 1). Write one sentence: &quot;Most people believe ___, but actually ___.&quot;</li><li>☐ Classify your idea (Concept 1). Is it 0 to 1 or 1 to n? Write two sentences explaining why.</li><li>☐ Draft a three-year plan (Concept 2). One page on where the company should be and the bets it takes to get there.</li><li>☐ Redefine your market honestly (Concept 3). Describe the real market customers pay for, and list your actual alternatives.</li><li>☐ Pick your first niche (Concept 4). Name the small group you could dominate first, and the next ring you&#x27;d expand into.</li><li>☐ Find your power-law priority (Concept 5). Name the one customer, feature or channel that matters more than the rest.</li><li>☐ Start a secrets list (Concept 6). Write down three things you believe are true that most people around you don&#x27;t.</li><li>☐ Have the foundation talk (Concept 7). With your co-founders, agree on roles, equity split and vesting, in writing.</li><li>☐ Score the seven questions (Concept 8). Rate your startup 1–5 on each and pick the weakest to work on this month.</li></ul><h3>Discussion questions</h3><p>Use these in a workshop, a founder meetup or with your co-founders.</p><ul><li>Thiel praises monopolies. Where is the line between a company that dominates because it&#x27;s much better and one that dominates by blocking others? Who should decide?</li><li>Is &quot;start small and monopolize&quot; realistic for a campus startup? What&#x27;s a market small enough to own at your school?</li><li>Thiel argues that a flawed plan beats having none. Lean startup advocates (see Vol. 3) stress fast iteration. Can both be right?</li><li>Thiel says success isn&#x27;t mostly luck. What role do you think luck, timing and privilege play, and what&#x27;s still in a founder&#x27;s control?</li><li>What is a &quot;secret&quot; you&#x27;ve noticed as a student that a company could be built on?</li><li>Thiel recommends low founder salaries and equity-heavy pay. Who can afford to work that way, and what does that mean for who becomes a founder?</li></ul><h3>Keep in mind</h3><p>Zero to One is one investor&#x27;s strongly argued point of view, not settled fact. Critics argue that Thiel underrates the role of luck, that his praise of monopoly overlooks harm to customers and competitors, and that his generalizations about countries are too broad. Read it as a set of sharp questions to test your thinking, not as rules.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li>The book itself, especially Chapters 1, 5, 9 and 13 if you&#x27;re short on time.</li><li>Peter Thiel, &quot;Competition Is for Losers,&quot; The Wall Street Journal, September 2014. A short essay version of Concept 3.</li><li>Blake Masters&#x27; published notes from Thiel&#x27;s 2012 Stanford course (CS183: Startup), the original lectures the book grew out of.</li><li>Next in the series: The Founder&#x27;s Shelf, Vol. 2: The Mom Test. Once you&#x27;ve found your secret, Rob Fitzpatrick shows you how to test it with real customers.</li></ul><h3>How to cite this book</h3><p>APA: Thiel, P., with Masters, B. (2014). Zero to one: Notes on startups, or how to build the future. Crown Business.</p><p>MLA: Thiel, Peter, with Blake Masters. Zero to One: Notes on Startups, or How to Build the Future. Crown Business, 2014.</p><p>Quotations in this guide are brief and attributed by chapter. All other text is an original summary by Campus Connect Academy, written for educational use.</p></section>"}, "v02": {"title": "The Mom Test", "lede": "Rob Fitzpatrick on customer conversations that tell the truth — Campus Connect Academy", "toc": ["About this volume", "Concept 1: The three rules of the Mom Test", "Concept 2: Spot bad data: compliments, fluff and ideas", "Concept 3: Ask the important questions, including the scary ones", "Concept 4: Keep it casual", "Concept 5: Commitment and advancement", "Concept 6: Finding and framing conversations", "Concept 7: Choose your customers, then run the process", "Put it to work"], "html": "<section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s1\" data-label=\"About this volume\"><h2>About this volume</h2><p>The core lesson of The Mom Test is that customers will mislead you about your idea, usually to be kind, so you have to ask questions even a loving mom can&#x27;t lie to you about. This volume covers the book&#x27;s seven main ideas and how to use them in your first customer conversations.</p><p>About the series. The Founder&#x27;s Shelf is a Campus Connect Academy series. Each volume turns one essential business book into practical lessons for student founders. Vol. 1 (Thiel) was about finding an idea worth building. This volume is about testing that idea with real people.</p><p>About the author. Rob Fitzpatrick is a founder who went through Y Combinator and built several companies before writing The Mom Test (2013). The book&#x27;s full subtitle sums it up: How to talk to customers and learn if your business is a good idea when everyone is lying to you. It&#x27;s short (about 130 pages) and is used widely in accelerators and university startup courses.</p><p>How to read this doc. Each section covers one concept, with the chapter it comes from. The explanations and example questions are our own summaries and wording, not text from the book. Read the book itself for Fitzpatrick&#x27;s full examples and conversation transcripts.</p><p>Each concept ends with For student founders, a short note on how to apply it right away.</p></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s2\" data-label=\"Concept 1: The three rules of the Mom Test\"><h2>Concept 1: The three rules of the Mom Test</h2><p>Chapter 1, &quot;The Mom Test&quot;</p><p>The book&#x27;s title comes from a simple scenario. If you ask your mom whether your business idea is good, she&#x27;ll say yes, because she loves you. Her answer tells you nothing. Fitzpatrick&#x27;s point is that almost everyone acts a bit like your mom: they don&#x27;t want to hurt your feelings, so they give you encouraging but useless answers.</p><p>The fix is not to stop asking. It&#x27;s to ask questions that produce honest, useful answers even from someone who wants to be nice. Fitzpatrick calls a question that passes this bar one that passes the Mom Test, and he boils it down to three rules (paraphrased):</p><ul><li>Talk about their life, not your idea. Once you describe your idea, people react to it politely. Ask about how they currently handle the problem instead.</li><li>Ask about specific things they&#x27;ve done in the past, not opinions or guesses about the future. People are bad at predicting what they&#x27;d do. They&#x27;re much more reliable describing what they actually did last week.</li><li>Talk less and listen more. The goal is to learn, not to persuade. If you&#x27;re talking most of the time, you&#x27;re pitching.</li></ul><h3>Bad questions vs. good questions</h3><p>The examples below are our own, written in the spirit of the book.</p><table><tr><td>Fails the Mom Test</td><td>Passes the Mom Test</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Do you think this is a good idea?&quot;</td><td>&quot;Walk me through the last time you dealt with this problem.&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Would you buy a product that did X?&quot;</td><td>&quot;What do you use for this right now? What do you like and dislike about it?&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;How much would you pay for this?&quot;</td><td>&quot;How much does this problem cost you now, in money or time?&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Would you use an app that helps you study?&quot;</td><td>&quot;How did you study for your last midterm? What went wrong?&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Is this something you&#x27;d want?&quot;</td><td>&quot;Have you tried to solve this before? What did you try?&quot;</td></tr></table><p>Notice the pattern. The good questions are about the person&#x27;s real behavior. They don&#x27;t mention your solution at all.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Students tend to interview friends, roommates and classmates. They&#x27;re the people most likely to act like your mom. Use the three rules extra carefully with them, or find strangers in your target market.</li><li>Before any conversation, rewrite your questions so none of them mention your product.</li><li>After a conversation, check how much of the time you were talking. If it was more than about a third, you were probably pitching.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s3\" data-label=\"Concept 2: Spot bad data: compliments, fluff and ideas\"><h2>Concept 2: Spot bad data: compliments, fluff and ideas</h2><p>Chapter 2, &quot;Avoiding Bad Data&quot;</p><p>Even with good questions, conversations drift into information that feels useful but isn&#x27;t. Fitzpatrick names three kinds of bad data and how to handle each one.</p><table><tr><td>Bad data</td><td>What it sounds like (our examples)</td><td>What to do</td></tr><tr><td>Compliments</td><td>&quot;That&#x27;s so cool.&quot; &quot;I love it.&quot; &quot;You should totally do this.&quot;</td><td>Treat compliments as noise. Steer back to their life and past behavior.</td></tr><tr><td>Fluff</td><td>Generic claims (&quot;I usually...&quot;), future promises (&quot;I would definitely...&quot;) and hypotheticals (&quot;I might...&quot;)</td><td>Anchor it to something concrete: &quot;When was the last time that happened? What did you do?&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Ideas</td><td>Feature requests: &quot;You should add a calendar.&quot;</td><td>Don&#x27;t just add it to the list. Dig into why they want it and what problem it would solve.</td></tr></table><h3>Why compliments are dangerous</h3><p>Compliments feel like validation, which is exactly why they&#x27;re risky. Fitzpatrick warns that founders often leave a meeting feeling great and having learned nothing. A useful test: if you walked away with a compliment but no new facts about the person&#x27;s problem, the meeting didn&#x27;t work.</p><h3>Dig beneath requests and emotions</h3><p>When someone asks for a feature or reacts strongly, the request itself isn&#x27;t the valuable part. The motivation behind it is. Our example questions for digging deeper:</p><ul><li>&quot;Why do you want that?&quot;</li><li>&quot;What would that let you do?&quot;</li><li>&quot;How are you coping without it now?&quot;</li><li>&quot;What else have you tried?&quot;</li></ul><h3>Stop pitching</h3><p>Fitzpatrick also flags a habit that ruins data: slipping into a pitch. Once you start describing your product enthusiastically, people shift into being polite. If you catch yourself pitching mid-conversation, stop and go back to asking about their experience.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>After each conversation, sort your notes into facts (things they did), compliments, fluff and ideas. Only the facts count as evidence.</li><li>When a classmate says &quot;I&#x27;d totally use that,&quot; treat it as fluff. Ask what they did the last time they had the problem.</li><li>Keep a list of feature requests, but next to each one write the underlying problem. The problem is what you design for.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s4\" data-label=\"Concept 3: Ask the important questions, including the scary ones\"><h2>Concept 3: Ask the important questions, including the scary ones</h2><p>Chapter 3, &quot;Asking Important Questions&quot;</p><p>A conversation can pass the Mom Test and still be a waste if it only covers safe, trivial topics. Fitzpatrick argues that every startup has a few questions that could prove the whole idea wrong, and that founders tend to avoid them because the answer might hurt.</p><h3>Look for the questions that could sink you</h3><p>A useful thought experiment from the book: imagine the company has already failed. What&#x27;s the most likely reason? The questions that would reveal that reason early are the ones to ask now. Examples of scary questions (our wording):</p><ul><li>&quot;Is this problem actually a priority for you, or just an annoyance?&quot;</li><li>&quot;Have you ever paid to solve it?&quot;</li><li>&quot;Who else would need to approve buying something like this?&quot;</li><li>&quot;What would make you stop using the tool you have now?&quot;</li></ul><h3>Love bad news</h3><p>Fitzpatrick encourages founders to welcome bad news. A clear &quot;no, this isn&#x27;t a problem for me&quot; saves you months of building the wrong thing. A lukewarm, polite answer is often worse, because it lets you keep hoping. He treats indifference as one of the most important signals: if people don&#x27;t care, that&#x27;s a real answer.</p><h3>Prepare your three big questions</h3><p>Before a set of conversations, Fitzpatrick recommends deciding on the three most important things you need to learn from that type of person. Having them ready keeps you focused, and it means you&#x27;ll still learn something useful even from a short chat. The three can change as you learn more.</p><h3>Zoom out before you zoom in</h3><p>He also warns against diving straight into the details of your solution. Start broad to confirm that the problem matters to this person, then narrow in. Otherwise you can get detailed feedback on a problem nobody actually cares about.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Write your pre-mortem: list three reasons your startup could fail. Turn each into a question you could ask a customer this week.</li><li>Keep a card or note with your three big questions and look at it before every conversation.</li><li>When you hear &quot;meh,&quot; don&#x27;t argue or re-explain. Write it down as an honest data point.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s5\" data-label=\"Concept 4: Keep it casual\"><h2>Concept 4: Keep it casual</h2><p>Chapter 4, &quot;Keeping It Casual&quot;</p><p>Formal meetings make people guarded. Fitzpatrick argues that the best customer learning often happens in quick, relaxed conversations that don&#x27;t feel like interviews at all.</p><p>When you announce &quot;I&#x27;d like to interview you about my startup,&quot; people shift into performance mode. They give careful, polite answers and expect you to pitch. A casual conversation about their work or their problem, without labeling it as research, gets more honest answers and takes far less of their time.</p><p>His practical advice, summarized:</p><ul><li>Drop the formality. You don&#x27;t always need a scheduled meeting, a deck or a script. A five-minute chat at an event or in a hallway can be enough.</li><li>Lead with the problem, not the product. Open with a question about their experience, not &quot;Let me tell you about my idea.&quot;</li><li>Keep it short. If you&#x27;ve prepared your three big questions, a quick conversation can answer one of them.</li><li>Save formal meetings for later. Once you&#x27;re further along and asking someone to commit, a proper meeting makes sense (Concept 5).</li></ul><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Campus is full of casual openings: club meetings, office hours, dining halls, events. Use them to ask one good question.</li><li>Instead of &quot;Can I interview you for my startup?&quot;, try something like &quot;I&#x27;m curious how you handle ___. How did that go last time?&quot;</li><li>Track these quick chats too. A two-minute conversation with a good question is real data.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s6\" data-label=\"Concept 5: Commitment and advancement\"><h2>Concept 5: Commitment and advancement</h2><p>Chapter 5, &quot;Commitment and Advancement&quot;</p><p>A meeting only really succeeds if the other person commits to something that costs them. Fitzpatrick argues that commitment is the clearest sign of real interest, far more than enthusiasm or praise.</p><h3>Three currencies of commitment</h3><p>Fitzpatrick describes commitment as someone giving up something they value. He identifies three main currencies:</p><table><tr><td>Currency</td><td>Examples (ours)</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>Agreeing to a follow-up meeting, trying a prototype, giving detailed feedback, meeting your team</td></tr><tr><td>Reputation</td><td>Introducing you to their boss, a colleague or a customer; giving a public testimonial</td></tr><tr><td>Money</td><td>Pre-ordering, paying a deposit, signing a letter of intent, joining a paid pilot</td></tr></table><p>Advancement is the next step in a customer&#x27;s path toward buying, and commitment is what moves them forward. A good meeting ends with a clear next step. A meeting that ends with &quot;This is great, keep me posted&quot; usually means the person isn&#x27;t really interested, however positive they sound.</p><h3>Watch for zombie leads</h3><p>Fitzpatrick warns about leads that look alive but never move: people who always say nice things but never commit to a next step. Founders often count these as wins. His advice is to ask for a specific commitment, and if the person won&#x27;t give one, accept that this is the answer.</p><h3>Find early evangelists</h3><p>The people who commit quickly, sometimes even before your product is finished, are especially valuable. They have the problem, know they have it, may already be trying to solve it, and have the budget or authority to buy. These are the customers to build around first.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>End every conversation by asking for something small but real: a follow-up, a trial, an introduction or a pre-order.</li><li>In your tracker, mark whether each conversation produced a commitment. Count commitments, not compliments.</li><li>If someone says they love it but won&#x27;t do anything, move them to the &quot;zombie&quot; column and spend your time on people who act.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s7\" data-label=\"Concept 6: Finding and framing conversations\"><h2>Concept 6: Finding and framing conversations</h2><p>Chapter 6, &quot;Finding Conversations&quot;</p><p>You don&#x27;t need a big network to get customer conversations. Fitzpatrick lays out several ways to find people, and a simple structure for asking them to talk.</p><h3>Where conversations come from</h3><ul><li>Cold outreach. It works, but it has a low hit rate. The goal of a cold message is usually just to earn one warm conversation.</li><li>Seize serendipity. Many problems come up naturally in everyday life. If you hear someone complain about the problem, ask about it then and there.</li><li>Find a good excuse. Having a reason to reach out, like researching a topic or writing about it, makes people more willing to talk.</li><li>Go where the customers are. Events, meetups, online communities and industry gatherings put many target customers in one place.</li><li>Be the expert. Hosting events, writing about the topic or teaching it brings people to you.</li><li>Use warm intros. Introductions from friends, advisers, professors and investors are the most reliable route. Ask everyone you know who they know.</li></ul><h3>The framing format</h3><p>When you ask for a conversation, Fitzpatrick suggests a simple structure. Our summary of the five parts:</p><ul><li>Vision: briefly say what you&#x27;re trying to improve in the world.</li><li>Framing: say where you are in the process and what you&#x27;re not asking (for example, you&#x27;re not selling anything yet).</li><li>Weakness: name the specific problem you&#x27;re stuck on, which shows you need their help.</li><li>Pedestal: explain why they in particular can help.</li><li>Ask: make a clear, specific request for help.</li></ul><p>The example below is our own, written for a student founder:</p><p>Example (ours): &quot;Hi Maya, I&#x27;m working on making it easier for student clubs to manage events. I&#x27;m early and just trying to learn, not selling anything. I&#x27;m stuck understanding how clubs handle check-ins at big events. You&#x27;ve run some of the biggest events on campus, so you&#x27;d know better than anyone. Could I ask you a few questions over coffee this week?&quot;</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Make a list of 20 people in your target market and how you could reach each one: a club, a class, a friend of a friend, a professor.</li><li>Use the framing format for every outreach message. Save a template and adjust it for each person.</li><li>Ask every person you talk to: &quot;Who else should I talk to about this?&quot; Each conversation can lead to the next.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s8\" data-label=\"Concept 7: Choose your customers, then run the process\"><h2>Concept 7: Choose your customers, then run the process</h2><p>Chapters 7–8, &quot;Choosing Your Customers&quot; and &quot;Running the Process&quot;</p><p>If you talk to everyone, your data turns to mush. Fitzpatrick argues that founders need to pick a narrow, specific customer segment, and then treat customer learning as a team process, not a solo hobby.</p><h3>Slice your segment</h3><p>A segment like &quot;students&quot; or &quot;small businesses&quot; is too broad. Different people inside it have different problems, so their answers contradict each other and you can&#x27;t tell what&#x27;s true. Fitzpatrick recommends slicing the segment until you have a group of people you can actually find and who share the same problem.</p><p>A simple way to test a segment is to ask two questions: Who exactly are these people, and where can you find them? If you can&#x27;t say where to find them, the segment is still too vague.</p><table><tr><td>Too broad</td><td>Better</td><td>Specific enough to start</td></tr><tr><td>College students</td><td>Students in clubs</td><td>Treasurers of student clubs at SDSU who handle dues</td></tr><tr><td>Small businesses</td><td>Local restaurants</td><td>Family-owned restaurants near campus that do catering</td></tr><tr><td>People who want to get fit</td><td>New gym members</td><td>First-year students who joined the rec center this semester</td></tr></table><p>(Examples are ours.)</p><h3>Run the process as a team</h3><p>Fitzpatrick warns against one founder becoming the &quot;customer person&quot; who keeps all the learning in their head. If only one person hears customers, the rest of the team ends up relying on secondhand opinions. His recommendations, summarized:</p><ul><li>Prep: before conversations, the team agrees on the three big questions and what you already believe.</li><li>Talk: take good notes during or right after each conversation, capturing exact facts and quotes from the customer.</li><li>Review: after a batch of conversations, the whole team reviews the notes together and updates its beliefs and plans.</li></ul><p>He also suggests a quick notation system so notes are easy to scan later, for example marking pains, goals, obstacles, workarounds and emotions with symbols. The specific symbols matter less than using a consistent system.</p><h3>How many conversations is enough?</h3><p>Fitzpatrick&#x27;s view is that you can stop when you&#x27;ve stopped hearing new things. If several conversations in a row tell you what you already know, it&#x27;s time to act on what you&#x27;ve learned.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Write your segment as &quot;who&quot; plus &quot;where.&quot; If you can&#x27;t fill in &quot;where,&quot; keep narrowing.</li><li>Create a shared doc or spreadsheet for all customer notes, so every co-founder can see them.</li><li>Hold a 30-minute review with your team after every 5–10 conversations.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s9\" data-label=\"Put it to work\"><h2>Put it to work</h2><p>These exercises turn this volume into a first round of real customer conversations. You can complete all of them in about two weeks.</p><h3>Student founder action plan</h3><ul><li>☐ Define your segment (Concept 7). Write &quot;who&quot; and &quot;where&quot; in one sentence each.</li><li>☐ Write your three big questions (Concept 3). Include at least one question you&#x27;re afraid to ask.</li><li>☐ Rewrite your questions (Concept 1). Remove every mention of your product. Make each one about a specific past event.</li><li>☐ Draft your outreach message (Concept 6). Use the five-part framing format.</li><li>☐ Have five conversations (Concept 4). Keep at least two of them casual.</li><li>☐ Sort your notes (Concept 2). Mark each note as fact, compliment, fluff or idea.</li><li>☐ Ask for a commitment (Concept 5). End each conversation with a specific next step, and record whether you got one.</li><li>☐ Hold a team review (Concept 7). What did you learn, and what will you change?</li></ul><h3>Conversation tracker template</h3><p>Copy this table into your own doc to track conversations.</p><table><tr><td>Date</td><td>Person (role)</td><td>Segment</td><td>Key facts learned</td><td>Commitment?</td><td>Next step</td></tr></table><h3>Discussion questions</h3><ul><li>Think of a time someone praised an idea of yours but never used it. What was really going on?</li><li>Why are friends and family especially unreliable as early customers? Is there any situation where they&#x27;re useful?</li><li>What is the scariest question you could ask about your startup idea? What would you do if the answer were bad?</li><li>Which commitment currency (time, reputation or money) is easiest to ask a student for? Which is the strongest signal?</li><li>How does the Mom Test connect to Thiel&#x27;s idea of secrets in Vol. 1? Can customer conversations reveal a secret?</li></ul><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li>The book itself. It&#x27;s short enough to read in an afternoon, and Fitzpatrick&#x27;s example conversations are the most useful part.</li><li>Steve Blank, The Startup Owner&#x27;s Manual (with Bob Dorf), for the broader method of customer development that the Mom Test fits into.</li><li>Next in the series: The Founder&#x27;s Shelf, Vol. 3: Build, Measure, Learn. Once you&#x27;ve learned from customers, Eric Ries shows how to turn that learning into experiments.</li></ul><h3>How to cite this book</h3><p>APA: Fitzpatrick, R. (2013). The mom test: How to talk to customers &amp; learn if your business is a good idea when everyone is lying to you. CreateSpace Independent Publishing Platform.</p><p>MLA: Fitzpatrick, Rob. The Mom Test: How to Talk to Customers &amp; Learn If Your Business Is a Good Idea When Everyone Is Lying to You. CreateSpace Independent Publishing Platform, 2013.</p><p>All text in this guide is an original summary by Campus Connect Academy, written for educational use. Example questions and scenarios are our own unless stated otherwise.</p></section>"}, "v03": {"title": "Build, Measure, Learn", "lede": "Eric Ries on launching before you're ready — Campus Connect Academy", "toc": ["About this volume", "Concept 1: A startup is an experiment, and its output is learning", "Concept 2: Find your leap-of-faith assumptions, then test them", "Concept 3: The minimum viable product", "Concept 4: The Build-Measure-Learn loop", "Concept 5: Innovation accounting and vanity metrics", "Concept 6: Pivot or persevere", "Concept 7: Small batches, engines of growth and the Five Whys", "Put it to work"], "html": "<section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s1\" data-label=\"About this volume\"><h2>About this volume</h2><p>The core lesson of The Lean Startup is that a startup&#x27;s real job is to learn what customers want as quickly and cheaply as possible, and that you learn by running experiments, not by writing long plans. This volume covers the book&#x27;s seven main ideas and how a student founder can apply them.</p><p>About the series. The Founder&#x27;s Shelf is a Campus Connect Academy series that turns essential business books into practical lessons for student founders. Vol. 2 (The Mom Test) covered learning from conversations. This volume covers learning from experiments and real behavior.</p><p>About the author. Eric Ries is an entrepreneur who co-founded IMVU, an online avatar and chat company, and later founded the Long-Term Stock Exchange. He developed the Lean Startup method from his own startup mistakes, drawing on Steve Blank&#x27;s customer development and on lean manufacturing ideas from Toyota. The Lean Startup was published by Crown Business in 2011.</p><p>How to read this doc. Each section covers one concept and the chapter it comes from. The explanations are our own summaries, not text from the book. Read the book for Ries&#x27;s full stories and case studies.</p><p>Each concept ends with For student founders, a note on how to apply it now.</p></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s2\" data-label=\"Concept 1: A startup is an experiment, and its output is learning\"><h2>Concept 1: A startup is an experiment, and its output is learning</h2><p>Chapters 2–3, &quot;Define&quot; and &quot;Learn&quot;</p><p>Ries defines a startup by uncertainty, not by size or industry. In his view, a startup is any organization trying to create a new product or service when it&#x27;s very unclear what will work. That can include a team inside a large company, a nonprofit program or a student project.</p><p>Because the uncertainty is so high, traditional management tools don&#x27;t fit. A detailed five-year plan assumes you already know the customer, the product and the market. A startup doesn&#x27;t. So Ries argues that its most important output isn&#x27;t revenue or code at first. It&#x27;s learning.</p><h3>Validated learning</h3><p>Ries calls the right kind of learning validated learning: learning that is backed by evidence from real customer behavior, not by opinions or guesses. The test is whether the evidence would convince a skeptic.</p><p>He illustrates this with his own experience at IMVU. The team spent months building a product feature they were sure customers wanted: integration with existing instant-messaging networks. When they launched, customers didn&#x27;t care about it at all. In hindsight, Ries realized they could have learned that in days with a simple test, instead of spending months building.</p><p>The lesson is to measure progress by what you&#x27;ve proven about customers, not by how much you&#x27;ve built.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>At the end of each week, write down one thing you&#x27;ve proven about your customers, not just something you&#x27;ve built or believe.</li><li>Treat your startup like a series of lab experiments. Each one should have a hypothesis and a result.</li><li>Notice when you&#x27;re building for months without testing anything with real users. That&#x27;s the IMVU trap.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s3\" data-label=\"Concept 2: Find your leap-of-faith assumptions, then test them\"><h2>Concept 2: Find your leap-of-faith assumptions, then test them</h2><p>Chapters 4–5, &quot;Experiment&quot; and &quot;Leap&quot;</p><p>Every startup plan rests on a few beliefs that have to be true for the business to work. Ries calls these leap-of-faith assumptions, and he argues that a founder&#x27;s first job is to name them and test them.</p><h3>The two biggest assumptions</h3><p>Ries highlights two that almost every startup depends on:</p><ul><li>The value hypothesis: does the product actually deliver value to customers once they use it?</li><li>The growth hypothesis: how will new customers discover the product, and will growth continue?</li></ul><p>If the value hypothesis is wrong, people won&#x27;t stick around. If the growth hypothesis is wrong, not enough people will ever arrive.</p><h3>Go and see for yourself</h3><p>Ries borrows a Toyota principle often translated as &quot;go and see.&quot; Instead of relying on reports or assumptions, founders should observe customers directly, where the problem actually happens. This fits closely with the customer conversations in Vol. 2.</p><h3>Experiments can be very simple</h3><p>Ries&#x27;s favorite example of a cheap experiment is the early story of Zappos. Before building inventory or warehouses, founder Nick Swinmurn tested whether people would buy shoes online. He photographed shoes at local stores, posted them on a simple website, and when someone ordered a pair, he went back to the store and bought them at full price. The business lost money on each sale, but it tested the key assumption: would customers buy shoes without trying them on?</p><p>The lesson is that an experiment doesn&#x27;t have to look like a finished company. It has to answer a specific question with real behavior.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Write your value hypothesis and growth hypothesis in one sentence each.</li><li>List your three riskiest assumptions. Rank them by how badly the business fails if each one is wrong.</li><li>For the riskiest one, design a test you could run this week for under $50.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s4\" data-label=\"Concept 3: The minimum viable product\"><h2>Concept 3: The minimum viable product</h2><p>Chapter 6, &quot;Test&quot;</p><p>The minimum viable product (MVP) is the simplest version of your product that lets you start learning from real customers. Ries stresses that &quot;minimum&quot; refers to the effort needed to learn, not to a stripped-down product you hope people will love.</p><p>Many founders misunderstand the MVP as &quot;version 1.0, but smaller.&quot; Ries&#x27;s point is different. An MVP is a tool for testing an assumption. Sometimes that&#x27;s a basic product, and sometimes it isn&#x27;t a product at all.</p><h3>Types of MVP from the book</h3><table><tr><td>Type</td><td>How it works (our summary)</td><td>Example in the book</td></tr><tr><td>Video MVP</td><td>Show a demo video of the product before it fully exists and measure interest.</td><td>Dropbox&#x27;s early demo video drew a flood of sign-ups for its waiting list.</td></tr><tr><td>Concierge MVP</td><td>Deliver the service by hand for a few customers, as a personal service.</td><td>Food on the Table, a meal-planning startup, served its first customer in person, week by week.</td></tr><tr><td>Wizard of Oz MVP</td><td>Customers think they&#x27;re using automated software, but humans do the work behind the scenes.</td><td>Aardvark, a question-answering service, had people answering queries before building full automation.</td></tr></table><h3>Quality and embarrassment</h3><p>Ries admits MVPs can feel embarrassing, because they&#x27;re rough. His argument is that early customers, the ones most eager for a solution, often tolerate an imperfect product. And if you don&#x27;t know who the customer is yet, you don&#x27;t know what &quot;quality&quot; means to them.</p><p>He also addresses common fears: that a competitor will steal the idea, that the brand will be damaged, or that a bad result will be discouraging. His response is that most ideas are hard to steal, you can test under a different name if needed, and early failure is far cheaper than late failure.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Ask: what&#x27;s the smallest thing I could put in front of a real user to test my riskiest assumption?</li><li>Consider a concierge MVP. As a student, you can often deliver a service by hand to five classmates before writing any code.</li><li>A landing page with a sign-up form, a Google Form or an Instagram post can all be valid MVPs.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s5\" data-label=\"Concept 4: The Build-Measure-Learn loop\"><h2>Concept 4: The Build-Measure-Learn loop</h2><p>Introduced in Part One and developed through Part Two</p><p>The Build-Measure-Learn loop is the engine of the Lean Startup. Ries describes it as a cycle that startups repeat as fast as possible:</p><ul><li>Build: turn an idea into something real, usually an MVP.</li><li>Measure: see how customers actually respond, using data.</li><li>Learn: decide what the data means, and whether to keep going or change course.</li></ul><p>Then the loop starts again with a new or refined idea.</p><h3>Plan the loop backward</h3><p>One of Ries&#x27;s key insights is that you should plan the loop in reverse. First decide what you need to learn. Then work out what you&#x27;d need to measure to learn it. Only then decide what to build to get that measurement. Planning this way stops you from building things that won&#x27;t teach you anything.</p><h3>Speed is the goal</h3><p>The aim is to shorten the total time it takes to get around the loop. A team that runs ten small experiments in a month learns far more than a team that spends the month on one big launch. Ries argues this is how startups beat larger, better-funded competitors.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Before you build anything, write: &quot;We want to learn ___. We&#x27;ll know by measuring ___. So we&#x27;ll build ___.&quot;</li><li>Time your loop. How many days does it take you to go from idea to result? Try to cut it in half.</li><li>Use the academic calendar to your advantage. Plan one full loop between now and finals, then another before the next semester.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s6\" data-label=\"Concept 5: Innovation accounting and vanity metrics\"><h2>Concept 5: Innovation accounting and vanity metrics</h2><p>Chapter 7, &quot;Measure&quot;</p><p>Ries argues that startups need a different kind of accounting, one that measures whether you&#x27;re actually learning and improving rather than just growing totals. He calls it innovation accounting.</p><h3>Three steps</h3><ul><li>Set a baseline. Use an MVP to measure where you really are today: sign-up rate, retention, how often people pay.</li><li>Tune the engine. Run experiments aimed at improving those numbers, one change at a time.</li><li>Decide. If the numbers improve toward a working business model, keep going. If they don&#x27;t, it&#x27;s time to consider a pivot (Concept 6).</li></ul><h3>Vanity metrics vs. actionable metrics</h3><p>Ries warns about vanity metrics: numbers that look good and go up over time but don&#x27;t tell you whether a specific change worked. Total registered users and total page views are classic examples. They can rise even when the product is getting worse.</p><p>Actionable metrics show cause and effect. Two tools Ries recommends:</p><ul><li>Cohort analysis: compare groups of users who joined at different times. Did people who signed up in March behave better than those who signed up in February, after you changed the product?</li><li>Split tests (A/B tests): show two versions to two groups at the same time and compare results.</li></ul><p>He also suggests that good metrics meet three tests, often called the three A&#x27;s (our summary):</p><table><tr><td>Test</td><td>What it means</td></tr><tr><td>Actionable</td><td>The metric shows a clear link between an action you took and the result.</td></tr><tr><td>Accessible</td><td>Everyone on the team can understand it and get to it easily.</td></tr><tr><td>Auditable</td><td>The data is trustworthy and can be checked, ideally against real customers.</td></tr></table><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Replace &quot;total sign-ups&quot; with a rate: out of every 100 visitors, how many sign up? Out of every 100 sign-ups, how many come back a week later?</li><li>Track results by week or by cohort, so you can see whether changes actually helped.</li><li>Before you celebrate a number on social media, ask whether it would tell you what to do next.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s7\" data-label=\"Concept 6: Pivot or persevere\"><h2>Concept 6: Pivot or persevere</h2><p>Chapter 8, &quot;Pivot (or Persevere)&quot;</p><p>A pivot, in Ries&#x27;s sense, is a structured change of course that tests a new fundamental hypothesis while keeping what you&#x27;ve already learned. It&#x27;s not giving up, and it&#x27;s not random change.</p><h3>Decide on a schedule</h3><p>Ries recommends holding regular pivot-or-persevere meetings, for example every few weeks or months. At each one, the team looks honestly at the data from its experiments and decides whether the current strategy is working. Without these check-ins, startups tend to drift for too long in what he calls the land of the living dead: not failing fast enough to stop, not succeeding enough to grow.</p><p>He tells the story of Votizen, a civic-engagement startup that pivoted several times, changing its target customer and product, before finding a model that worked.</p><h3>Kinds of pivot</h3><p>Ries describes ten kinds of pivot. Our summary:</p><table><tr><td>Pivot</td><td>What changes</td></tr><tr><td>Zoom-in</td><td>One feature becomes the whole product.</td></tr><tr><td>Zoom-out</td><td>The whole product becomes one feature of something bigger.</td></tr><tr><td>Customer segment</td><td>Same product, different customer.</td></tr><tr><td>Customer need</td><td>Same customer, different problem to solve.</td></tr><tr><td>Platform</td><td>From an app to a platform, or the reverse.</td></tr><tr><td>Business architecture</td><td>Between high-margin, low-volume and low-margin, high-volume models.</td></tr><tr><td>Value capture</td><td>A different way of making money.</td></tr><tr><td>Engine of growth</td><td>A different main way of growing (Concept 7).</td></tr><tr><td>Channel</td><td>A different way of reaching customers.</td></tr><tr><td>Technology</td><td>A different technology for the same solution.</td></tr></table><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Put a pivot-or-persevere review on the calendar now, for example at the end of each month or semester.</li><li>Before the review, decide which numbers would count as &quot;working.&quot; Setting the bar in advance stops you from rationalizing.</li><li>When you pivot, write down what you learned that you&#x27;re keeping. That&#x27;s what makes it a pivot rather than a restart.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s8\" data-label=\"Concept 7: Small batches, engines of growth and the Five Whys\"><h2>Concept 7: Small batches, engines of growth and the Five Whys</h2><p>Chapters 9–11, &quot;Batch,&quot; &quot;Grow&quot; and &quot;Adapt&quot;</p><p>Part Three of the book is about speeding up once you&#x27;re learning. Three ideas stand out.</p><h3>Work in small batches</h3><p>Ries uses an example of stuffing envelopes for a mailing. The intuitive approach is to fold all the letters, then stuff all the envelopes, then seal them all. In practice, doing one complete envelope at a time is often just as fast, and you find problems (like letters that don&#x27;t fit) right away instead of at the end.</p><p>The startup version: release small changes often instead of one big launch. At IMVU, the team practiced continuous deployment, shipping small updates many times a day, with automatic checks that stopped the process when something broke. Small batches mean faster feedback and smaller mistakes.</p><h3>Pick your engine of growth</h3><p>Ries says sustainable growth comes from one of three engines (our summary):</p><table><tr><td>Engine</td><td>How it grows</td><td>What to watch</td></tr><tr><td>Sticky</td><td>Customers stay for a long time, so growth comes from adding more customers than you lose.</td><td>Retention and churn: new customers must exceed customers leaving.</td></tr><tr><td>Viral</td><td>Using the product spreads it to others.</td><td>Each user should bring in more than one new user on average.</td></tr><tr><td>Paid</td><td>You pay to acquire customers through ads or sales.</td><td>Each customer must be worth more than it costs to acquire them.</td></tr></table><p>Most startups should focus on one engine at a time.</p><h3>Ask &quot;why&quot; five times</h3><p>When something goes wrong, Ries recommends the Five Whys, a technique from Toyota. Ask why the problem happened, then why that cause happened, and so on, about five levels deep. This usually reveals a human or process cause, not just a technical one. The fix should then be proportional: small problems get small fixes at each level.</p><p>Ries also warns about the &quot;five blames&quot;: using the exercise to find someone to blame instead of a process to fix.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Break your next launch into the smallest piece you could ship this week.</li><li>Decide which engine of growth fits your startup, and pick the one number that tracks it.</li><li>After your next mistake, run a Five Whys with your co-founders. Agree in advance that the goal is fixing systems, not blaming people.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s9\" data-label=\"Put it to work\"><h2>Put it to work</h2><p>These exercises take you through one full Build-Measure-Learn loop. Plan for two to three weeks.</p><h3>Student founder action plan</h3><ul><li>☐ Write your two hypotheses (Concept 2). One sentence each for value and growth.</li><li>☐ Rank your leap-of-faith assumptions (Concept 2). Pick the riskiest one.</li><li>☐ Plan the loop backward (Concept 4). What will you learn, measure and build?</li><li>☐ Build an MVP (Concept 3). A video, landing page, concierge service or simple prototype.</li><li>☐ Set your baseline (Concept 5). Measure two or three actionable metrics, not vanity metrics.</li><li>☐ Run one experiment (Concept 5). Change one thing and compare results.</li><li>☐ Hold a pivot-or-persevere review (Concept 6). Decide your bar for &quot;working&quot; first.</li><li>☐ Choose your engine of growth (Concept 7). Write down the one number that tracks it.</li></ul><h3>Experiment card template</h3><p>Copy this for each experiment.</p><table><tr><td>Field</td><td>Your answer</td></tr><tr><td>Assumption being tested</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>What we&#x27;ll build (MVP)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Metric we&#x27;ll measure</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Result that would prove it right</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Result that would prove it wrong</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Date we&#x27;ll decide</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>What we learned</td><td></td></tr></table><h3>Discussion questions</h3><ul><li>Thiel (Vol. 1) argues that a flawed plan beats no plan. Ries argues for constant experimenting. Where do they agree, and where do they really disagree?</li><li>What&#x27;s the difference between a pivot and quitting? Have you seen a student project pivot well?</li><li>Which vanity metrics do student startups (and clubs) tend to brag about? What would the actionable version be?</li><li>When could an MVP hurt you, for example in health, finance or safety-critical products?</li><li>Which engine of growth fits campus products best, and why?</li></ul><h3>Keep in mind</h3><p>Lean methods are widely used, but they have critics. Some argue that constant testing can push founders toward small, safe improvements rather than bold ideas, which is close to Thiel&#x27;s critique in Vol. 1. Others note that some products, such as hardware or medical devices, can&#x27;t be tested quickly or cheaply. Use the method as a toolkit, not a religion.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li>The book itself, especially Chapters 3, 6, 7 and 8.</li><li>Steve Blank, The Four Steps to the Epiphany, the customer development method that inspired much of the Lean Startup.</li><li>Eric Ries, The Startup Way (2017), which applies these ideas inside larger organizations.</li><li>Next in the series: The Founder&#x27;s Shelf, Vol. 4: What Job Are You Hired For? Clayton Christensen on why customers really buy.</li></ul><h3>How to cite this book</h3><p>APA: Ries, E. (2011). The lean startup: How today&#x27;s entrepreneurs use continuous innovation to create radically successful businesses. Crown Business.</p><p>MLA: Ries, Eric. The Lean Startup: How Today&#x27;s Entrepreneurs Use Continuous Innovation to Create Radically Successful Businesses. Crown Business, 2011.</p><p>All text in this guide is an original summary by Campus Connect Academy, written for educational use. Tables and examples are our own unless stated otherwise.</p></section>"}, "v04": {"title": "What Job Are You Hired For?", "lede": "Clayton Christensen on why customers really buy, and how small players beat giants — Campus Connect Academy", "toc": ["About this volume", "Concept 1: Sustaining vs. disruptive innovation", "Concept 2: Why good companies fail", "Concept 3: How small players win", "Concept 4: Customers hire products to do a job", "Concept 5: The milkshake story", "Concept 6: Jobs have functional, social and emotional sides", "Concept 7: Organize around the job", "Put it to work"], "html": "<section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s1\" data-label=\"About this volume\"><h2>About this volume</h2><p>Clayton Christensen&#x27;s work answers two questions every founder faces: how can a small startup beat a giant, and why do customers really choose one product over another? This volume covers his two most useful ideas for students, disruptive innovation and jobs to be done.</p><p>About the series. The Founder&#x27;s Shelf is a Campus Connect Academy series that turns essential business books into practical lessons for student founders. Vols. 2 and 3 covered learning from customers. This volume gives you a sharper lens for what you&#x27;re learning.</p><p>About the author. Clayton Christensen (1952–2020) was a professor at Harvard Business School and one of the most influential management thinkers of his time. This volume draws on two of his books:</p><ul><li>The Innovator&#x27;s Dilemma (Harvard Business School Press, 1997), which introduced the theory of disruptive innovation.</li><li>Competing Against Luck (HarperBusiness, 2016), written with Taddy Hall, Karen Dillon and David S. Duncan, which lays out the jobs-to-be-done theory.</li></ul><p>How to read this doc. Concepts 1–3 come from The Innovator&#x27;s Dilemma and Concepts 4–7 from Competing Against Luck. The explanations are our own summaries, not text from the books.</p><p>Each concept ends with For student founders, a note on how to apply it now.</p></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s2\" data-label=\"Concept 1: Sustaining vs. disruptive innovation\"><h2>Concept 1: Sustaining vs. disruptive innovation</h2><p>The Innovator&#x27;s Dilemma</p><p>Christensen splits innovation into two types, and the difference explains why some startups topple industry leaders.</p><table><tr><td></td><td>Sustaining innovation</td><td>Disruptive innovation</td></tr><tr><td>What it is</td><td>Makes an existing product better for existing customers</td><td>Starts with a product that&#x27;s worse on traditional measures but simpler, cheaper or more convenient</td></tr><tr><td>Who it serves</td><td>The best, most demanding customers</td><td>Customers the leaders ignore, or people who weren&#x27;t buying at all</td></tr><tr><td>Who usually wins</td><td>Established companies</td><td>New entrants</td></tr><tr><td>Examples from his research</td><td>Each new generation of a larger, faster disk drive for the same customers</td><td>Smaller disk drives that first served new markets, like personal computers</td></tr></table><p>The key point is that &quot;disruptive&quot; doesn&#x27;t mean &quot;high-tech&quot; or &quot;revolutionary.&quot; A disruptive product often looks inferior at first, which is why established companies dismiss it.</p><h3>The disk drive study</h3><p>Christensen built his theory by studying the disk drive industry in detail. Each time a new, smaller drive size appeared (for example, moving from 8-inch to 5.25-inch drives), it was initially worse for the leading companies&#x27; main customers. So the leaders kept improving their existing products. Meanwhile, newcomers sold the smaller drives to new markets. As the new drives improved, they took over the mainstream market, and most of the old leaders failed.</p><p>He found the same pattern in other industries. Two of his best-known examples:</p><ul><li>Excavators: hydraulic excavators started as small, weak machines for niche jobs, while cable-operated machines handled big excavation. Hydraulics improved and eventually displaced most of the cable-excavator makers.</li><li>Steel: minimills, which make steel from scrap, started with low-quality products like rebar. They gradually moved up to higher-quality steel and took share from the large integrated mills.</li></ul><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Ask whether your product is sustaining (better for existing customers) or disruptive (simpler and cheaper for people the leaders ignore).</li><li>If you&#x27;re competing head-on with a big company for its best customers, you&#x27;re playing a sustaining game, which incumbents usually win.</li><li>Look for customers who find existing products too expensive, complicated or overbuilt. That&#x27;s often where disruption starts.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s3\" data-label=\"Concept 2: Why good companies fail\"><h2>Concept 2: Why good companies fail</h2><p>The Innovator&#x27;s Dilemma</p><p>The &quot;dilemma&quot; in the title is that great companies can fail because they&#x27;re well managed. Christensen found that the leaders that lost to disruption weren&#x27;t lazy or careless. They listened to their best customers, invested in the highest-margin products and did what business schools teach. Those sensible choices are exactly what made them ignore disruptive products.</p><h3>Five principles of disruption</h3><p>Christensen lays out five principles that explain the pattern. Our summary:</p><ul><li>Customers and investors control resources. Companies end up investing in what their current customers and shareholders want, which is rarely an inferior product for a small market.</li><li>Small markets don&#x27;t solve big companies&#x27; growth needs. A billion-dollar company needs huge new markets to keep growing, so a tiny emerging market looks unimportant.</li><li>Markets that don&#x27;t exist yet can&#x27;t be analyzed. Disruptive markets are new, so there&#x27;s no data on them. Companies that require solid market research before investing can&#x27;t move.</li><li>A company&#x27;s capabilities also define what it can&#x27;t do. The processes and values that make a company great at its core business make it bad at something very different, such as a low-margin, low-volume product.</li><li>Technology can improve faster than customers need. Products often improve beyond what most customers can use. When that happens, a simpler, cheaper product becomes &quot;good enough&quot; and starts winning.</li></ul><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Principle 2 is your advantage. A market too small for a big company to care about can be big enough to launch a startup.</li><li>Principle 5 is a search tool. Look for products people complain are too complicated, expensive or full of features they never use.</li><li>Don&#x27;t assume big companies will notice you early. Often they rationally choose not to.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s4\" data-label=\"Concept 3: How small players win\"><h2>Concept 3: How small players win</h2><p>The Innovator&#x27;s Dilemma, with ideas from Christensen&#x27;s later work</p><p>Disruption usually starts with a foothold where incumbents don&#x27;t want to compete. In later work, especially The Innovator&#x27;s Solution (2003, with Michael Raynor), Christensen described two main kinds of foothold:</p><table><tr><td>Foothold</td><td>Who the first customers are</td><td>Typical example</td></tr><tr><td>Low-end</td><td>Existing customers who are overserved: they&#x27;d happily take a simpler, cheaper product</td><td>Steel minimills starting with rebar</td></tr><tr><td>New-market</td><td>People who weren&#x27;t buying at all, because existing products were too expensive or too hard to use</td><td>Early personal computers reaching people who couldn&#x27;t use mainframes</td></tr></table><p>From the foothold, the disruptor improves step by step and moves up toward mainstream customers. By the time the leader takes it seriously, the disruptor is often too far ahead.</p><h3>How incumbents can respond</h3><p>Christensen&#x27;s advice to large companies was to create a separate, independent unit to pursue the disruptive product, sized to the small market and free from the main business&#x27;s priorities. He cites IBM&#x27;s decision to build its personal computer business in a separate location from its mainframe operations as an example.</p><h3>A word of caution</h3><p>&quot;Disruption&quot; became a buzzword, and Christensen himself pushed back on how loosely it was used. In a 2015 Harvard Business Review article with Raynor and Rory McDonald, he argued that not every successful startup is disruptive in his sense. Uber, for instance, didn&#x27;t start at the low end or with nonconsumers, so they didn&#x27;t consider it a classic disruption.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Identify your foothold. Are you starting with overserved customers (low-end) or people who aren&#x27;t buying anything today (new-market)?</li><li>Map your path upmarket. What improvements would let you serve the next, more demanding group of customers?</li><li>Use &quot;disruptive&quot; carefully in pitches. Investors who know the theory will ask whether you really fit it.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s5\" data-label=\"Concept 4: Customers hire products to do a job\"><h2>Concept 4: Customers hire products to do a job</h2><p>Competing Against Luck</p><p>Christensen&#x27;s second big idea is that people don&#x27;t simply buy products. They hire them to get a job done in their lives, and they fire them when something else does the job better.</p><p>In the jobs-to-be-done view, a job is the progress a person is trying to make in a particular circumstance. The focus moves away from the customer&#x27;s demographics (age, income, major) and the product&#x27;s features, and toward the situation and the goal.</p><p>Christensen often credited an older idea from Harvard marketing professor Theodore Levitt: people don&#x27;t really want a drill, they want the hole it makes. Jobs-to-be-done pushes that further. The hole is a step toward something else, such as hanging a shelf or making a room feel finished.</p><h3>Why this matters</h3><p>The book argues that many innovations fail because companies rely on correlations: customers who look alike, or features that seem popular. Those correlations don&#x27;t explain why someone buys. Understanding the job explains the cause, which makes success less a matter of luck. That&#x27;s where the title comes from.</p><table><tr><td>Traditional question</td><td>Jobs-to-be-done question</td></tr><tr><td>Who is our customer?</td><td>What progress is this person trying to make?</td></tr><tr><td>What features do customers want?</td><td>What job are they hiring a product for?</td></tr><tr><td>Who are our competitors in our category?</td><td>What else could they hire for this job, including doing nothing?</td></tr><tr><td>How do we make the product better?</td><td>What&#x27;s getting in the way of the job being done well?</td></tr></table><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Rewrite your customer description as a job: &quot;When I&#x27;m ___, I want to ___, so I can ___.&quot;</li><li>Stop describing your target user only by demographics like &quot;Gen Z college students.&quot; Describe the situation they&#x27;re in when they need you.</li><li>In customer conversations (Vol. 2), ask what they hired before your product, and why they fired it.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s6\" data-label=\"Concept 5: The milkshake story\"><h2>Concept 5: The milkshake story</h2><p>Competing Against Luck</p><p>The most famous example of jobs-to-be-done involves a fast-food chain trying to sell more milkshakes. Our summary of the story as Christensen tells it:</p><p>The chain had already done traditional research. It asked customers who fit its milkshake-buyer profile what would make the shake better: thicker, cheaper, more chocolatey. It made changes based on the answers, and sales didn&#x27;t improve.</p><p>Christensen&#x27;s team took a different approach. They spent time in restaurants observing who bought milkshakes, when and in what situation. A surprising share of shakes were bought early in the morning by people alone, who left right away with only a shake.</p><p>When researchers asked these customers what job they were hiring the shake for, a pattern emerged. They had a long, boring drive to work. They wanted something to keep them occupied with one free hand, and they wanted to stay full until lunch. A thick milkshake was ideal: it took a long time to drink through a straw and wasn&#x27;t messy.</p><p>That changed how the company should see its competition. For this job, the milkshake wasn&#x27;t competing with other chains&#x27; milkshakes. It was competing with bagels, bananas, doughnuts, coffee and even boredom.</p><p>Later in the day, the same product was often hired for a completely different job: a parent buying a treat for a child. That job called for a different product, such as a smaller shake that could be finished quickly.</p><h3>The lessons</h3><ul><li>The circumstance matters more than the customer. The same person can hire different products in different situations.</li><li>Competition is defined by the job, not the category. Your real competitors may look nothing like you.</li><li>Observation beats asking for features. Watching real behavior revealed what surveys missed.</li></ul><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Spend an hour watching people use a product in your space, without interviewing anyone. Note the time, place and situation.</li><li>List everything your target user might hire instead of your product, including doing nothing or using a spreadsheet.</li><li>Check whether your product is being hired for more than one job. Each job might need a different design or message.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s7\" data-label=\"Concept 6: Jobs have functional, social and emotional sides\"><h2>Concept 6: Jobs have functional, social and emotional sides</h2><p>Competing Against Luck</p><p>A job is rarely only practical. Christensen and his co-authors stress that every job has three dimensions, and the non-practical ones often decide the purchase.</p><table><tr><td>Dimension</td><td>The question it answers</td><td>Example (ours): choosing a laptop for college</td></tr><tr><td>Functional</td><td>What practical task needs doing?</td><td>Run class software, last a full day on battery</td></tr><tr><td>Social</td><td>How do I want others to see me?</td><td>Look professional in a meeting, fit in with classmates</td></tr><tr><td>Emotional</td><td>How do I want to feel?</td><td>Feel prepared and in control, not anxious about deadlines</td></tr></table><h3>Compete with nonconsumption</h3><p>Often the biggest competitor isn&#x27;t another product. It&#x27;s nonconsumption: people who have the job but aren&#x27;t hiring anything, because every option is too expensive, complicated or inconvenient. They put up with the problem or use a workaround.</p><p>The book highlights Southern New Hampshire University as an example. It built a large online program around the job of working adults who wanted a degree to move ahead in their careers but couldn&#x27;t attend a traditional campus. Many of them had been nonconsumers of higher education. Designing around their job, including fast, personal responses when they first inquired, helped the program grow dramatically.</p><h3>The big hire and the little hire</h3><p>The authors also distinguish between the big hire, when someone buys a product, and the little hire, each time they actually use it. A product can win the big hire and still fail if people stop using it. A gym membership bought in January and never used is a classic example.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>For your product, fill in all three rows of the functional, social and emotional table. Make sure your marketing speaks to all of them.</li><li>Look for nonconsumers: people who have your problem and use nothing, or a messy workaround. That group is often easier to win than a competitor&#x27;s customers.</li><li>Track the little hire. Measure how often people actually use your product after they sign up.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s8\" data-label=\"Concept 7: Organize around the job\"><h2>Concept 7: Organize around the job</h2><p>Competing Against Luck</p><p>Once you understand the job, the book argues that your company should be organized to do that job well from start to finish.</p><h3>Build the whole experience</h3><p>Customers judge the entire experience of getting the job done, not just the product. That includes how they find you, buy, start using the product and get help. The authors argue that companies win when they design that whole experience around the job, even when it means doing things outside their usual area.</p><h3>Don&#x27;t let data hide the job</h3><p>The authors warn that businesses often measure what&#x27;s easy, like sales by product or by demographic, and lose sight of the job. Over time, teams can start optimizing those numbers instead of serving the customer&#x27;s real goal. Their advice is to keep the job as the main reference point when making decisions.</p><h3>A job statement makes it concrete</h3><p>Many jobs-to-be-done practitioners write the job as a single statement. A format widely used today (ours to share here, not from the book) is:</p><p>When [situation], I want to [motivation], so I can [expected outcome].</p><p>Example (ours): &quot;When I&#x27;m studying alone late at night before an exam, I want to quickly check whether I understand the material, so I can feel confident and get some sleep.&quot;</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Write a job statement for your product and share it with your team. Use it to settle debates about features.</li><li>Map the customer&#x27;s full experience, from discovering you to getting the job done. Find the step where people drop off.</li><li>Review your metrics. Does at least one of them measure whether the job actually got done?</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s9\" data-label=\"Put it to work\"><h2>Put it to work</h2><p>These exercises apply both of Christensen&#x27;s theories to your own startup. Plan on about a week.</p><h3>Student founder action plan</h3><ul><li>☐ Classify your innovation (Concept 1). Sustaining or disruptive? Write two sentences on why.</li><li>☐ Find your foothold (Concept 3). Low-end, new-market or neither?</li><li>☐ Look for overshooting (Concept 2). List products in your space that are too complex or expensive for many users.</li><li>☐ Write your job statement (Concept 7). Use the &quot;When... I want to... so I can...&quot; format.</li><li>☐ Observe the circumstance (Concept 5). Watch people in the situation where the job arises.</li><li>☐ List what else gets hired (Concept 5). Include workarounds and doing nothing.</li><li>☐ Map all three dimensions (Concept 6). Functional, social and emotional.</li><li>☐ Find the nonconsumers (Concept 6). Who has the job but uses nothing today?</li></ul><h3>Discussion questions</h3><ul><li>Pick a product you use daily. What job do you hire it for? What did you fire to hire it?</li><li>Is your startup idea sustaining or disruptive? What would a big company in your space have to do to stop you?</li><li>Which campus problems have lots of nonconsumers, people who just put up with the problem?</li><li>Christensen argued that well-managed companies fail by listening too closely to their best customers. Fitzpatrick (Vol. 2) says to listen to customers carefully. How do you reconcile those?</li><li>Why might the emotional side of a job matter more to students than the functional side?</li></ul><h3>Keep in mind</h3><p>Disruption theory is influential but debated. Some researchers, most notably historian Jill Lepore in a 2014 New Yorker essay, have questioned how well Christensen&#x27;s case studies support the theory and how reliably it predicts which companies fail. Jobs-to-be-done is also practiced in several competing versions. Treat both as lenses for thinking, not guarantees.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li>The Innovator&#x27;s Dilemma, especially the opening chapters on disk drives.</li><li>Competing Against Luck, especially the milkshake and Southern New Hampshire University chapters.</li><li>Clayton M. Christensen, Michael E. Raynor and Rory McDonald, &quot;What Is Disruptive Innovation?&quot; Harvard Business Review, December 2015.</li><li>Christensen also wrote How Will You Measure Your Life? (2012), applying his theories to careers and personal life.</li><li>Next in the series: The Founder&#x27;s Shelf, Vol. 5: Be a Purple Cow. Seth Godin on making something worth talking about.</li></ul><h3>How to cite these books</h3><p>APA:</p><p>Christensen, C. M. (1997). The innovator&#x27;s dilemma: When new technologies cause great firms to fail. Harvard Business School Press.</p><p>Christensen, C. M., Hall, T., Dillon, K., &amp; Duncan, D. S. (2016). Competing against luck: The story of innovation and customer choice. HarperBusiness.</p><p>MLA:</p><p>Christensen, Clayton M. The Innovator&#x27;s Dilemma: When New Technologies Cause Great Firms to Fail. Harvard Business School Press, 1997.</p><p>Christensen, Clayton M., et al. Competing Against Luck: The Story of Innovation and Customer Choice. HarperBusiness, 2016.</p><p>All text in this guide is an original summary by Campus Connect Academy, written for educational use. Tables, job statements and examples are our own unless stated otherwise.</p></section>"}, "v05": {"title": "Be a Purple Cow", "lede": "Seth Godin on making something worth talking about — Campus Connect Academy", "toc": ["About this volume", "Concept 1: Be remarkable", "Concept 2: Safe is risky, and very good isn't enough", "Concept 3: Market to the people who care", "Concept 4: Marketing is helping people make a change", "Concept 5: The smallest viable market", "Concept 6: People like us, status and tension", "Concept 7: Position by picking extremes, and earn permission", "Put it to work"], "html": "<section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s1\" data-label=\"About this volume\"><h2>About this volume</h2><p>Seth Godin&#x27;s core lesson is that you can&#x27;t buy attention anymore, so you have to build something people choose to talk about, and then serve a specific group of people who care. This volume covers seven ideas from two of his books.</p><p>About the series. The Founder&#x27;s Shelf is a Campus Connect Academy series that turns essential business books into practical lessons for student founders. Vol. 4 explained why customers buy. This volume is about how they find you.</p><p>About the author. Seth Godin is a marketer, entrepreneur and one of the most widely read business bloggers. He founded Yoyodyne, an early online marketing company acquired by Yahoo, where he then served as vice president of direct marketing. He later founded altMBA, an online leadership workshop. He has written more than 20 books. This volume draws on two:</p><ul><li>Purple Cow: Transform Your Business by Being Remarkable (Portfolio, 2003)</li><li>This Is Marketing: You Can&#x27;t Be Seen Until You Learn to See (Portfolio, 2018)</li></ul><p>How to read this doc. Concepts 1–3 come mainly from Purple Cow, and Concepts 4–7 mainly from This Is Marketing. The explanations are our own summaries, not text from the books.</p><p>Each concept ends with For student founders, a note on how to apply it now.</p></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s2\" data-label=\"Concept 1: Be remarkable\"><h2>Concept 1: Be remarkable</h2><p>Purple Cow</p><p>Godin opens the book with a road trip through France. At first, his family was delighted by the cows grazing beside the road. After a while, they stopped noticing them. Cows, however pretty, become boring once you&#x27;ve seen enough. But a purple cow would get everyone&#x27;s attention.</p><p>That&#x27;s the book&#x27;s whole argument in one picture. Most products are brown cows: competent, pleasant and invisible. A Purple Cow is a product that is remarkable, which Godin defines literally: worth making a remark about.</p><h3>Why advertising stopped working</h3><p>Godin describes an era he calls the TV-industrial complex. For decades, companies could make an average product for the masses, buy lots of TV ads and grow. He argues that era ended because:</p><ul><li>Consumers have more choices than ever, and less time.</li><li>People have learned to ignore ads.</li><li>Most needs are already met by existing products.</li></ul><p>When people ignore ads and already have what they need, marketing has to be built into the product itself. If the product isn&#x27;t worth talking about, no amount of promotion will save it.</p><h3>Remarkable isn&#x27;t the same as better</h3><p>Remarkable doesn&#x27;t necessarily mean higher quality. It can mean being the fastest, the cheapest, the most extreme, the most beautiful, the strangest or the most personal. What matters is that it stands out enough that people want to tell others.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Ask one honest question: if a customer used your product today, would they bring it up unprompted with a friend? If not, what would make them?</li><li>Look for one dimension where you could be extreme rather than slightly better on every dimension.</li><li>Treat &quot;worth talking about&quot; as a product requirement, not a marketing task you&#x27;ll handle later.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s3\" data-label=\"Concept 2: Safe is risky, and very good isn&#x27;t enough\"><h2>Concept 2: Safe is risky, and very good isn&#x27;t enough</h2><p>Purple Cow</p><p>Godin flips the usual idea of risk. Many founders and companies choose the safe path: a product that won&#x27;t offend anyone, looks like what already exists and appeals to the average customer. Godin argues that this is actually the riskiest choice, because a safe product is a boring product, and boring products get ignored.</p><p>He makes a related point about quality. A product that is merely very good is, in his view, a problem, because very good is now the minimum. Plenty of competitors are very good. Being very good just puts you in the crowd of brown cows.</p><h3>Design for the edges</h3><p>Godin&#x27;s alternative is to design for the edges of the market rather than the middle. The edges are where people care intensely about something: the fastest, the cheapest, the most luxurious, the most sustainable. Products that win the edges get noticed, and their reputation can spread toward the middle over time.</p><table><tr><td>Safe, middle-of-the-road thinking</td><td>Purple Cow thinking</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal to everyone</td><td>Delight a specific group intensely</td></tr><tr><td>Avoid criticism</td><td>Accept that some people won&#x27;t like it</td></tr><tr><td>Be a bit better than competitors</td><td>Be different in a way people notice</td></tr><tr><td>Add marketing after the product is built</td><td>Build what&#x27;s remarkable into the product</td></tr><tr><td>Copy what&#x27;s working for others</td><td>Ask what nobody in your category is doing</td></tr></table><p>(Table is our summary.)</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Ask what your product would look like if you pushed one feature, price or experience to an extreme.</li><li>Expect some people to dislike it. If nobody could possibly object, it&#x27;s probably not remarkable.</li><li>Find the boring default in your category, such as the usual club recruitment flyer, and do the opposite.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s4\" data-label=\"Concept 3: Market to the people who care\"><h2>Concept 3: Market to the people who care</h2><p>Purple Cow, building on Godin&#x27;s earlier Unleashing the Ideavirus</p><p>You don&#x27;t need everyone to notice you. You need the right few, who will then tell everyone else.</p><p>Godin draws on the idea, popularized by Geoffrey Moore, that new products spread through a population in stages: innovators and early adopters first, then the mainstream majority, then the laggards. The mainstream mostly ignores new things until people it trusts recommend them. So a remarkable product should aim first at the people who actively look for new things.</p><h3>Find your sneezers</h3><p>Godin calls the people who spread ideas sneezers, a term from his earlier book Unleashing the Ideavirus (2000). Sneezers are the friends everyone asks for recommendations, the people who post about new finds, the ones whose opinions others trust. He also describes the otaku, a Japanese word he uses to mean an intense, almost obsessive enthusiasm for a category. People with otaku for something will seek out remarkable products in it and talk about them.</p><p>The strategy is to:</p><ul><li>Identify the people in your market who care most and spread ideas.</li><li>Build something remarkable specifically for them.</li><li>Make it easy for them to share it.</li></ul><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>List the 10 people on campus who are most likely to try your product first and tell others. What do they care about?</li><li>Build a reason to share into the product: a referral, something visual, an experience worth posting.</li><li>Don&#x27;t try to reach the whole campus at launch. Win one passionate group, such as a club, a dorm or a major, and let it spread from there.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s5\" data-label=\"Concept 4: Marketing is helping people make a change\"><h2>Concept 4: Marketing is helping people make a change</h2><p>This Is Marketing</p><p>In This Is Marketing, Godin redefines marketing. In his view, it isn&#x27;t about tricking people or shouting louder. It&#x27;s the generous work of helping specific people solve a problem and become who they want to be.</p><p>Every purchase, he argues, is really about a change. A customer moves from one state to another: from overwhelmed to organized, from out of shape to fit, from unknown to respected. Good marketing starts by understanding the change the customer wants, then shows how the product helps make it happen.</p><h3>Start with empathy</h3><p>Godin emphasizes that marketers have to see the world the way the customer sees it, including their fears, dreams and beliefs, even when those differ from yours. He frames this as an act of empathy and service, not manipulation.</p><h3>A simple marketing statement</h3><p>Godin suggests a short exercise for getting clear on who you serve and what you promise. Here is a simplified version, in our own words:</p><p>Who it&#x27;s for: people who already believe ___. Who we focus on first: the ones who want ___. What they get: ___.</p><p>Example (ours): &quot;Our study app is for students who believe they can do well if they stay organized. We focus on students who want to stop cramming. We promise it will help them feel prepared a week before exams, not the night before.&quot;</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Write the change your customer wants as &quot;from ___ to ___.&quot; That&#x27;s your core message.</li><li>Fill in the marketing statement above and test it on five people in your target market.</li><li>Ask whether your marketing feels like a service to the customer. If it feels like a trick, rethink it.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s6\" data-label=\"Concept 5: The smallest viable market\"><h2>Concept 5: The smallest viable market</h2><p>This Is Marketing</p><p>Godin&#x27;s advice is to aim at the smallest group of people your work can serve well and still sustain itself. He calls this the smallest viable market.</p><p>It feels backward. Founders usually want the biggest possible market. Godin&#x27;s argument is that when you try to serve everyone, you end up serving no one particularly well, and nobody feels the product was made for them. When you pick a small, specific group, you can understand them deeply, make something that truly fits, and become the obvious choice for them.</p><h3>Why small first</h3><ul><li>Focus: you can learn exactly what this group needs and design for it.</li><li>Word of mouth: small groups talk to each other, so a hit spreads quickly within them.</li><li>Resilience: a group that loves you is more loyal than a big market that&#x27;s merely aware of you.</li><li>A base to grow from: once you own one group, you can expand to neighboring ones.</li></ul><p>This lines up closely with Thiel&#x27;s advice in Vol. 1 to start small and monopolize, and with Fitzpatrick&#x27;s advice in Vol. 2 to slice your customer segment.</p><h3>Who it&#x27;s for, and who it&#x27;s not for</h3><p>Godin encourages founders to be clear about who their product is not for. Saying no to some people makes the product more meaningful to the right people.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Define your smallest viable market in one sentence. How many people are in it, and is that enough to get started?</li><li>Write a line describing who your product is not for.</li><li>Check your messaging: would someone in your target group read it and think &quot;this is for me&quot;?</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s7\" data-label=\"Concept 6: People like us, status and tension\"><h2>Concept 6: People like us, status and tension</h2><p>This Is Marketing</p><p>Godin argues that people mostly decide what to buy based on who they are and who they want to be seen as, not on a rational list of features. Three ideas from the book explain how that works.</p><h3>Culture: &quot;people like us&quot;</h3><p>One of Godin&#x27;s best-known phrases is &quot;people like us do things like this.&quot; His point is that most choices are shaped by culture: the groups we belong to and the norms they share. People buy a certain kind of laptop, join a certain gym or wear certain brands partly because that&#x27;s what people like them do.</p><p>For marketers, this means the goal often isn&#x27;t to convince individuals one at a time. It&#x27;s to become part of what a particular group sees as normal for people like them.</p><h3>Status</h3><p>Godin describes status as a person&#x27;s sense of where they stand relative to others in a group, and he argues it drives far more behavior than we admit. He distinguishes between two kinds of status seeking (our summary):</p><ul><li>Affiliation: wanting to belong and be accepted within a group.</li><li>Dominance: wanting to be ahead of or above others.</li></ul><p>Different customers care about different kinds. A product that signals belonging appeals to one group; a product that signals being first or best appeals to another.</p><h3>Tension</h3><p>Godin notes that change is uncomfortable. Effective marketing creates a kind of tension: a feeling that you&#x27;re missing out or falling behind people like you. Taking action, such as buying, joining or signing up, resolves that tension. He stresses that this should be honest. The tension should point to a real change the product delivers.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Complete the sentence for your market: &quot;Students like us do things like ___.&quot; Is your product part of that, or fighting against it?</li><li>Decide whether your product signals belonging or standing out. Make sure your branding matches.</li><li>Notice which campus norms already favor your product, such as clubs, majors or social groups, and start there.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s8\" data-label=\"Concept 7: Position by picking extremes, and earn permission\"><h2>Concept 7: Position by picking extremes, and earn permission</h2><p>This Is Marketing, and Godin&#x27;s earlier Permission Marketing</p><p>To stand out, you have to choose where you stand. Godin offers a simple tool for positioning and an older idea about how to keep customers&#x27; attention once you have it.</p><h3>Pick two axes and go to the edge</h3><p>Godin suggests drawing a simple chart with two axes, each representing something your market cares about: price, speed, convenience, sustainability, exclusivity, ease of use and so on. Place every existing option on the chart. Then look for an empty corner, a spot at the extreme of both axes where no one else stands, that a meaningful group of people would value.</p><p>Example (ours) for campus food delivery:</p><table><tr><td>Option</td><td>Speed</td><td>Healthiness</td></tr><tr><td>Big delivery apps</td><td>Medium</td><td>Low to medium</td></tr><tr><td>Dining hall</td><td>Fast</td><td>Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Meal-prep services</td><td>Slow (weekly)</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Empty corner to target</td><td>Very fast</td><td>Very high</td></tr></table><p>The point isn&#x27;t to beat everyone on every dimension. It&#x27;s to be the clear choice for people who care about the two things you picked.</p><h3>Earn permission</h3><p>In Permission Marketing (1999), Godin argued that the most valuable marketing asset is permission: people who have agreed to hear from you. Instead of interrupting strangers with ads, you earn the right to talk to people by offering something worth their attention. He described good permission-based messages as having three qualities (our summary):</p><ul><li>Anticipated: people look forward to hearing from you.</li><li>Personal: the message is clearly meant for them.</li><li>Relevant: it&#x27;s about something they care about.</li></ul><p>An email list or a community of people who opted in is worth far more than a large audience that ignores you.</p><h3>For student founders</h3><ul><li>Draw your two-axis chart. Put your competitors on it, including workarounds, and find your empty corner.</li><li>Start building permission now: an email list, a group chat or an Instagram account of people who asked to hear from you.</li><li>Before sending any message to your list, check it against anticipated, personal and relevant.</li></ul></section><section class=\"vol-section\" id=\"vol-s9\" data-label=\"Put it to work\"><h2>Put it to work</h2><p>These exercises turn this volume into a launch marketing plan. Plan on about a week.</p><h3>Student founder action plan</h3><ul><li>☐ Run the remark test (Concept 1). Ask five users what they&#x27;d say about your product to a friend. Write down their exact words.</li><li>☐ Pick your extreme (Concept 2). Name the one dimension where you&#x27;ll be remarkable.</li><li>☐ List your sneezers (Concept 3). Name 10 people likely to try it first and tell others.</li><li>☐ Write the change (Concept 4). &quot;From ___ to ___.&quot;</li><li>☐ Define your smallest viable market (Concept 5). Include who it&#x27;s not for.</li><li>☐ Complete the &quot;people like us&quot; sentence (Concept 6) for your target group.</li><li>☐ Draw your positioning chart (Concept 7) and find your empty corner.</li><li>☐ Start a permission list (Concept 7) and get your first 50 opt-ins.</li></ul><h3>Discussion questions</h3><ul><li>What&#x27;s a recent product or brand that you told a friend about without being asked? What made it remarkable?</li><li>Godin says safe is risky. When is the safe choice actually the right one for a startup?</li><li>What are the &quot;people like us&quot; norms at your school? Which products ride those norms?</li><li>Is it ethical to use status and tension in marketing? Where&#x27;s the line between helping someone change and manipulating them?</li><li>How does Godin&#x27;s smallest viable market compare with Thiel&#x27;s &quot;start small and monopolize&quot; (Vol. 1)?</li></ul><h3>Keep in mind</h3><p>Godin writes in short, punchy, opinionated chapters, and some critics find his ideas more inspiring than measurable. Pair his thinking with the evidence-based methods in Vols. 2 and 3: use Godin to decide what to try, and lean testing to find out whether it works.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li>Purple Cow, a quick read of under 200 pages.</li><li>This Is Marketing, the more complete statement of his current thinking.</li><li>Permission Marketing (1999) and Tribes (2008), on earning attention and leading communities.</li><li>Godin&#x27;s daily blog, at seths.blog.</li><li>Next in the series: The Founder&#x27;s Shelf, Vol. 6: Never Split the Difference. Chris Voss on negotiating like an FBI agent.</li></ul><h3>How to cite these books</h3><p>APA:</p><p>Godin, S. (2003). Purple cow: Transform your business by being remarkable. Portfolio.</p><p>Godin, S. (2018). This is marketing: You can&#x27;t be seen until you learn to see. Portfolio.</p><p>MLA:</p><p>Godin, Seth. Purple Cow: Transform Your Business by Being Remarkable. Portfolio, 2003.</p><p>Godin, Seth. This Is Marketing: You Can&#x27;t Be Seen Until You Learn to See. Portfolio, 2018.</p><p>All text in this guide is an original summary by Campus Connect Academy, written for educational use. The phrase &quot;people like us do things like this&quot; is Godin&#x27;s; tables and examples are our own.</p></section>"}};
